To find the magnetic field strength \( B \) at the center of a circular coil, we use the formula:

\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 I}{2r}
\]

where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength (in Tesla)
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of free space (\( 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T m/A} \))
- \( I \) is the current (in Amperes)
- \( r \) is the radius of the coil (in meters)

Given:
- Total turns \( N = 100 \)
- Current \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \)
- Radius \( r = 0.2 \, \text{m} \)

Substituting the values:

\[
B = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T m/A}) \times (1 \, \text{A})}{2 \times (0.2 \, \text{m})}
\]

\[
B = \frac{4\pi \times 10^{-7}}{0.4}
\]

\[
B = 10 \pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T}
\]

\[
B = 3.14 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \(\boxed{3.14 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{T}}\).
