","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law:  
\[
F = F_{\text{net}} = qE
\]  
where \(F\) is the gravitational force, \(q\) is the charge of the proton, and \(E\) is the electric field strength.  
\[
F = m \cdot a
\]  
Thus,  
\[
m \cdot a = qE \implies a = \frac{qE}{m}
\]  
Substituting the given values:  
\[
a = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg})} = 4.78 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
The magnitude of the acceleration of the proton is \(\boxed{4.78 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The acceleration \(a\) is constant, and the proton starts from rest, so \(v = 0\) at \(t = 0\). Using the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = a \cdot t
\]  
Solving for \(t\):  
\[
