","The entropy production rate \( \pi_S \) is given by:
\[
\pi_S = \frac{d\eta}{T}
\]
where \( \Delta \eta \) is the frictional work done, and \( T \) is the temperature. The frictional work done is:
\[
\Delta \eta = F_{\text{fr}} v
\]
Thus, the entropy production rate is:
\[
\pi_S = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v}{T}
\]
This is derived from the second law of thermodynamics, which states that the entropy change of a system is given by:
\[
\Delta S = \frac{\Delta \eta}{T}
\]
where \( \Delta \eta \) is the work done by the system and \( T \) is the temperature. The entropy production rate is the rate of change of entropy:
\[
\dot{\sigma} = \frac{\Delta \sigma}{\Delta t} = \frac{\Delta \eta}{\Delta t} / T = \frac{f \nu}{T}
\]
Thus, the entropy production rate is:
\[
\boxed{\frac{F_{\text{fr}} v}{T}}
